{"v":1,"st":"LA","y":"2022","L":{"cd":{"f":"2022-cd.json","n":6,"lbl":"U.S. House","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2022_us_cd118_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2022/shp/cb_2022_us_cd118_500k.zip","7d16875ca4d56f63402b122e37a47690a0b3edb30129b475b7d3e79191dcf774"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's lines of the 118th Congress's districts (its file says session 118).","chk":[5,5,0],"why":"","rec":["Act 5 of the 2022 First Extraordinary Session (H.B. 1, Schexnayder): six districts, one majority-Black","2022-11-08","Louisiana Legislature: H.B. 1, 2022 First Extraordinary Session (Act 5), bill history: vetoed 2022-03-10, veto overridden 2022-03-30","https://legis.la.gov/Legis/BillInfo.aspx?s=221ES&b=HB1&sbi=y","Vetoed 2022-03-10, veto overridden 2022-03-30 (House 72-31, Senate 27-11), effective 2022-03-31 for the regular 2022 congressional elections. A federal court enjoined it in June 2022 (Robinson v. Ardoin) but the U.S. Supreme Court stayed that order on 2022-06-28, so this map was used on 2022-11-08 (open primary; runoff 2022-12-10). Census file: the Bureau documents its 2022 files as the plans in effect for the 2022 election cycle. Cached files: cb_2022 cd118 differs from both cb_2020 (the 2011 plan) and cb_2024 (S.B. 8): its District 6 spans lon -91.7 to -90.15, lat 29.2 to 30.7."],"c":5,"lv":{"federal":5}},"sldu":{"f":"","n":0,"lbl":"State Senate","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2022_us_sldu_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2022/shp/cb_2022_us_sldu_500k.zip","a58879d76e18dd617823ed6725904933625bc34c5242061694557a6654681a8c"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2022 file of legislative districts, the lines it records for the 2022 legislative session year.","chk":[1,2,1],"why":"No map of Louisiana's upper chamber districts for 2022: the election was held on the Act 24 of the 2011 First Extraordinary Session (S.B. 1, Chaisson), still in force; the 2022 Act 1 (S.B. 1) was not yet in effect, and the Census Bureau published no file of those lines, so this page draws none. The official record of the plan is under The lines.","rec":["Act 24 of the 2011 First Extraordinary Session (S.B. 1, Chaisson), still in force; the 2022 Act 1 (S.B. 1) was not yet in effect","2011-10-22","Louisiana Legislature: S.B. 1, 2011 First Extraordinary Session (Act 24), bill history; signed 2011-04-14","https://legis.la.gov/Legis/BillInfo.aspx?s=111ES&b=SB1&sbi=y","Louisiana elects its Legislature in odd years (2011, 2015, 2019, 2023), so there is no regular legislative general election in this year; this is the plan in force for any special election and for all other purposes. Act 1 of 2022 took effect 'for the 2023 election' only and kept the 2011 districts until 10:00 a.m. on 2024-01-08. The Census 2022 file holds the 2022 Act's lines, not the 2011 lines still in force: cb_2022 and cb_2024 are identical for all 40 features (39 districts plus a placeholder), while cb_2020 shares only 2 with them. Census documents 2022 files as 'in effect for the 2022 election cycle', which here means the plan enacted in 2022 for the 2023 elections."]},"place":{"f":"2022-place.json","n":140,"lbl":"Cities, towns and villages","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2022_us_place_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2022/shp/cb_2022_us_place_500k.zip","f63a8defe119d0f117126d856565218f3218a8e229ce2e9a0d5c2cecc3d3281f"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2022 file (boundaries as of January 1, 2022).","chk":[],"why":"","c":426,"lv":{"city":419,"measure":7}},"sd":{"f":"2022-sd.json","n":66,"lbl":"School districts","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2022_us_unsd_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2022/shp/cb_2022_us_unsd_500k.zip","00670bdf5f82495dfaea6fadcfc19e3f2b6c11ab3fc7b143bcb005f8e6b1ae55"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2022 file (boundaries as of January 1, 2022).","chk":[],"why":"","c":332,"lv":{"measure":6,"school":326}}},"t":{"state":{"LA":["u-s-s